A||N||18||Call||Another question on a Fiero and Transmission||||5||
Z||000000||Call||01-16-2000||10:25 PM||||Well I found another Fiero fairly close to me and the ad is as follows:Silver,Gray Int.,V6, 4spd man,pwr wind,Cold A/C,CD plyr,spkrs in seats,new fuel pump,filter,tune up & plug wires, tagged & inspect 11/99, alloy wheels, new tires on rear, runs & drives but will be needing clutch soon.REDUCED TO 1500.00, CLUTCH DISC SLIPS COMPLETLY, NEED CASH! &lt;----Would this cost alot to replace by myself if I just bought a kit and also why I am replacing this could I put a 5 speed in and how much would that cost if I put it in myself? If I can put in a 5 spd. how would I accomplish this and how much does the 5 spd. kits cost and what type would I need to get and where could I get one? Thanks ahead of time!||||reg||5
Z||000001||Shiner||01-17-2000||12:28 AM||||Sounds like a great car.  I don't think you said what year, but it sounds like an '85 GT.  This is an aero-notchback.  <P> A fiero specific 5 speed Getrag is probably going to cost you as much as the car.  They are VERY in demand, because they only came on late '86/87/88 V6 cars.  The 4 speed muncie is a good trans though.  I would figure on about $200-$300 for the clutch, if you do it yourself, and depending on what exactly is worn out.  <P> Rodney makes a shift cable conversion for FWD 5 speed Getrags,  found in Chevy Berettas, amongst others.  <P> I would just fix the muncie in it now.  Clutches on fieros tend to have a short life, so you can usually get a GREAT deal on cars because of this.  I would offer the guy $1k straight up, if you think the car is worth it.  I would bet he would take that, maybe even less?  I got my '87 GT for $2000, and the owner was asking $3300.  The trans is worth almost what I paid for the car! hehe<P> Good luck, however you decide to go.  If you are in the area (Lehigh Valley, PA), I would LOVE to help you assess the car!||||reg||1
Z||000002||Call||01-17-2000||12:53 AM||||THanks for the reply, it is a 85' Fiero (2m6?) here is a pic of it tell me what you think    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">. Also how long do the clutches on Fieros usually last? <IMG SRC="http://www.angelfire.com/tx3/calli/FIERO2M6.jpg">   Also, are the 4 speed manuals faster than the automatics?<P>[This message has been edited by Call (edited 01-17-2000).]<p>[This message has been edited by Call (edited 01-17-2000).]||||reg||10
Z||000003||batboy||01-17-2000||01:01 AM||||If I were to pay that much for a Fiero with a bad clutch, the body and interior better be near perfect. Repair shops can often quote some pretty high figures when it comes to replacing Fiero clutches. Chances are this guy is trying to dump the car to cut his losses. Waiting works good in these cases. Chances are slim somebody else will buy it.<P>I bought my 85 GT for about that much last summer and it runs great. Clear coat on the paint is looking shabby, but I drive it 100 miles at least 4 times a week. There are still plenty of bargains out there if you're patient.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Ed||01-17-2000||01:06 AM||||The clutch on my '87 GT went 106,000 miles. "It" didn't fail, but the disc was oiled by a leaking rear camshaft cover seal. The disc itself looked good: I would guess it would have gone on a lot longer.  ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Call||01-17-2000||01:09 AM||||Well i was going to go ahead and replace the clutch myself to save on money instead of having a mechanic do the job, there is also one here that runs good for 1700 but i might be able to bring them down, I tried to contact them today but no-one answered, so ill try again tommorrow, does this car in the picture look like it is in good shape to anyone else?||||reg||11
Z||000006||batboy||01-17-2000||01:11 AM||||Oh my, it's my Batmobile's twin brother, a silver 85 SE complete with one of those funky luggage racks. Looks like it's in pretty good shape, but I bet the headlights need work since they're left in the up position. It's probably worth a grand in my opinion.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Greg Piet||01-17-2000||01:12 AM||||Looking at teh car I would say go for it.<BR>I bought an 85 SE wit the paint and interior in worse shape for the same price.  One thing stated above that does confuse me though.  My 85 SE has a 4 speed isuzu, which is a klunky tranny.  If you haev the 4 speed muncie thats great but i thought all 85's came with the isuzu||||reg||1
Z||000008||Call||01-17-2000||01:20 AM||||Not sure about the muncie tranny. Also, is there a way to take off the luggage rack on the deck lid? If so how would I accomplish this? I plan on offering $1000-$1200 for it. I figure the cd player and the speakers in the seat basically replace the cost of the clutch anyway  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.||||reg||5
Z||000009||mrfiero||01-17-2000||02:37 AM||||All '84 4-sp trannies and all '85-mid '86 V-6 4-sp. trannies were built by Muncie.<P>All '85-'88 4-cyl. 5-sp. trannies were built by Isuzu.<P>All mid '86-'88 V-6 5-sp. trannies were designed by Getrag and built by Muncie (known as a Muncie-Getrag 5-sp.).<P>There were no 4-sp. Isuzu trannies offered for the Fiero.<P>Your '85 SE V-6 has a Muncie 4-sp.  If it is a 4-cyl. '85 SE then it would have the Isuzu 5-sp. tranny.||||reg||3
Z||000010||DISenchanted||01-17-2000||05:22 AM||||Was a 4 speed an option on an 86gt?||||reg||5
Z||000011||bHooper||01-17-2000||07:24 AM||||the 4 speed trany was standard on all '85 and early '86 GT's.  the automatic was a option both years.  the v-6 cars did not have a 5 speed option until may of '86.||||reg||2
Z||000012||batboy||01-17-2000||09:18 AM||||The speakers in the seat were standard Fiero issue in the early models. Actually they don't usually sound all that good compared to the later model 4" X 6" speakers in the rear sail panels. You have to take the seat cover off the seat back to get to them and since they're small (3-1/2" I think) they can't handle much bass (but that's what subwoofers are for). CD players can be installed for $200 to $300.||||reg||1
Z||000013||stsmithgt||01-17-2000||10:03 AM||||Hey cool.  with my 86gt, my 4-sp is a muncie then.  I learned something today and it's only 7:00 a.m.. I geuss I can go home now the work day is over! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000014||Call||01-17-2000||12:14 PM||||Well I got some more info on the car, it isn't drivable because of the clutch but supposedly runs great, the headlights need a new motor, and of course it will need a new clutch, what do any of you guys think it is worth?||||reg||5
Z||000015||mshill||01-17-2000||12:51 PM||||call,  from the picture the car looks good, BUT...  1) the interior shot is from the passenger side, you can bet the drivers side seat isn't quite as nice, 2) the headlights are up, that can only indicate one thing...<P>Personally, if the car is not drivable, for whatever reason, you have to consider it a parts car.  You can have no idea what other problems it may have if you cannot drive it.  Case in point, I just paid $500 for an 86GT because it had a bad oil leak and a "bad clutch".  Couldn't drive it more than 100 yards to put it on the trailer.  Turns out the clutch is just a leaky master and the oil leak is at the valve covers, but it need another $1000 of work in the suspension, brakes and exhaust area.<P>If you can't drive it, you need to consider it a parts car, $500 max, unless you have another $2000 to throw at it.<P><BR>[This message has been edited by mshill (edited 01-17-2000).]<p>[This message has been edited by mshill (edited 01-17-2000).]||||reg||1
