Z||000017||Greg Piet||01-17-2000||01:34 PM||||This is mainly directed at mrfiero.<BR>I mean absolutely no disrespect, distrust, or any of those nasty things but I am having a problem with a 4 speed muncie in my Fiero. Reason being...<P>In my Pontiac 85 service manual they only have a listing for an auto, a 76mm 4 speed, and a 76mm 5 speed.<P>In the chilton manual they have a listing of an auto, a 76mm Isuzu 4 speed(MG1), a 76mm 5 speed isuzu(MT2), and a 5 speed 282 muncie (MG2).  Granted it seems odd that an Isuzu and a Muncie are denoted with a MG.  There is no mention in either manual of a 4 speed muncie since in the pontiac manual I am assuming both 76mm trannies are the isuzu(since the metric system would be more likely used to designate a tranny by Japan than the US).<BR> <BR>In either case if I am wrong about the isuzu in my 85, I apologize for all the threads where I have discussed my isuzu tranny when it is really a muncie.    ||||reg||1
Z||000018||bHooper||01-17-2000||02:27 PM||||Greg, I have done a lot of research, over the past year, trying to decide what to do with my 86.5 GT.  My car originally had the Muncie/Getrag 5 speed.  This has caused me much pain, due to the rarity of the trany, and the stupidity of the salvage yards.  Through this "education" process, I have learned the following... Muncie built the 4 speed trany (this was used in both 4 cyl and 6 cyl cars until 1986.5), and the Getrag 5 speed that was used on later production v-6 cars.  In 86, GM began using the 5 speed Isuzu trany on the 4 Cyl.  In all of the research, questioning, probing, and general p!$$ing people off, that I have done over the past year... I have found those to be the cold hard facts.  <P>So, if you have a 4 speed trany in your car... it is, without question, undeniably, a Muncie!  Unless, you or someone else has had the trany swapped!?<P>Oh, yea, I havent a clue as to who made the automatic!||||reg||1
